## 2. Chromium "never close the window"

Your research is right, and it confirms the limit: **no policy stops the window from closing when the last tab is closed**. So the fix is layered, and only the first layer lives in the backend repo (not this project):

| Layer | Mechanism | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Protected tab (already in the backend change set) | keep-alive tab that `close_tab` refuses + reopened on every connection | The **AI** can never orphan the window; the user closing everything else is harmless |
| 2. Enterprise policy (below) | `RestoreOnStartup` + `BackgroundModeEnabled` | Any window that opens **always** starts with a tab; no first-run dialog; background mode survives |
| 3. Supervisor (optional) | relaunch loop instead of `exec /init` | Even a manual "close window" (Alt+F4) brings Chromium back |

**Policy content** — this must be written inside the container by `DockerBrowserManager._build_run_command` (`codx/junior/browser/docker_browser_manager.py`, next to the existing `-v` mounts). That file is **not part of this project** (`codx/junior/` here only holds `engine/git_engine.py`), so I cannot patch it from this repo — hand this to the backend side:

1. Write `keep_open.json` into the session folder (`<session>/policies/keep_open.json`) next to `cdp_proxy.py`:

```json
{
  "BackgroundModeEnabled": true,
  "RestoreOnStartup": 4,
  "RestoreOnStartupURLs": ["about:blank"],
  "HideFirstRunExperience": true,
  "MetricsReportingEnabled": false
}
```

2. Mount it at **both** Linux policy locations (Chromium from Debian reads `/etc/chromium/policies/managed`, Chrome-for-Testing reads `/etc/opt/chrome/policies/managed`; the LinuxServer image's base varies, mounting both is harmless):

```
-v <session>/policies:/etc/chromium/policies/managed:ro
-v <session>/policies:/etc/opt/chrome/policies/managed:ro
```

3. Verify inside a session: open `chrome://policy` → the three keys must show *OK*.

**Two caveats you should know before wiring this:**

- `ExtensionInstallForcelist` with `dlnpfhfhmkiebpnlllpehlmklgdggbhn;https://google.com` requires the container to reach the Web Store **and** the extension's update manifest; in an offline/restricted container the force-install silently fails. If you want it, self-host the `.crx` + `update.xml` and point the forcelist at your own origin — otherwise rely on layers 1 and 3.
- `--kiosk` does hide the tab-close button, but it also removes the window chrome the user needs to switch tabs in the live view, so it is not a good fit for co-browsing.

Layer 3 (supervisor) replaces the current entrypoint payload `python3 /cdp_proxy.py & exec /init` with a loop that waits for the desktop session and relaunches Chromium when it exits — that is the only mechanism that survives a full window close, and it needs no policy or extension.

